  "query_preview": "..\nSynoptic Setup: Forecast begins with relatively zonal to NW flow\nfrom Montana through the PCNW and across SW Canada. To the S, hot\nhigh pressure sits over the desert southwest with a ridge \nbuilding into the Intermountain West. Farther West a highly \namplified trough is working its way into the Gulf of Alaska.  \n\nToday through Thursday night: Zonal to NW flow remains relatively\nintact with temps remaining 5 to 15 degrees above normal with \nrelatively dry conditions. There is a small chance (5-15%) during\nthis time that any fires over the PCNW could begin to pool smoke \nacross Montana for light haze, but current HRRR and RAP Smoke \nmodels suggest that it will not make it to eastern MT at all. \nMeanwhile, the Intermountain West ridge will drift east and the \ncrest will start to bump up against MT zonal/NW flow while the \nGulf of Alaska trough behind it will begin slamming up against \nthe PCNW coast. \n\nFriday: Ridge crest will move across eastern Montana raising \ntemps to 10 to 20 degrees above normal with highs into the 70s \nand 80s. Downslope winds could begin to lower RH a bit more than \nnormal as well here leading to possible fire weather concerns. \nCurrently winds speeds seem just under criteria. This ridge will \ncontinue be monitored for breakdown timing and any increase in \nwind speed. Meanwhile, the PCNW trough will smash into the large \nridge and break apart with the southern section digging S into the\nlower Intermountain west and southern California while the \nnorthern part progresses eastward allowing cool Canadian air to \nfill in behind it on the Plains. \n\nFriday night into Monday: Newly minted SW flow regime will drag a\ncold front across Montana Friday night and lead to some pacific \nmoisture increasing into the region. By Saturday to Saturday \nnight rains showers will begin to form as highs drop into the 40s \nand 50s by Sunday or nearly 20 degrees below normal. Expect a \nstrong NW t N wind behind this frontal passage Saturday and\nSaturday night. Lows are suggesting that snow may be possible \novernight Sunday night at higher elevations but totals will be \nless than an inch if it snows at all. Also, warm ground should \nmelt off any accumulations. Cooler, wetter periods hold through \nabout Monday before beginning to clear out. \n\nForecasts beyond Sunday come into question as ensemble data moves\nout of phase with each other. In addition, the southern Prairie ridge\nto the east appears to become persistent enough to block forward \nprogress of the southern flow which may means the cooler \nconditions stay a bit longer but dry conditions could return to\nMontana Monday evening through about mid-week. Expect these \nperiods to change in future updates. So, low confidence. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- No changes from NBM.\n\n- There is moderate to high confidence (50-70%) in frontal timing\n  Friday night/Saturday morning.\n\n- There is high confidence (80-100%) in warmer temperatures\n  through Friday and cooler temps this weekend.\n\n- There is moderate to high confidence (50-80%) in any\n  precipitation occuring between Saturday night and Monday\n  morning.",
